  { The deadlock detector, Go's checkdead (1.0.139). An M that has made
    its final look at every queue and is about to sleep, in epoll_wait
    or on its futex, counts itself in the low half of rt_msleep here;
    rt_msleepout takes it out again the moment it wakes and adds one to
    the high half. When this M is the last of the started ones to sleep
    (the low half equals rt_mstarted) nothing runs any more, and the
    program is dead if nothing can wake anything: no M spinning or being
    woken (rt_nspinning; rt_wakep claims it before it pops an M), no
    routine asleep on a timer (rt_sleeph), none parked on the poller
    (rt_npollwait, kept by rt_waitio and rt_acwait), and a routine still
    alive (main while it has not returned, or another one after). The
    reads are consistent because the word is read again at the end:
    with the same value nobody woke meanwhile, so every M was asleep
    throughout and nothing could have changed what was read; if one
    woke, that M looks again when it sleeps. A routine readied by
    another is never missed: whoever readied it either found it in its
    own final look or was still running; a readied routine sits in a
    queue only while some M is awake. The check costs nothing on the
    hot path: two locked adds an M sleep, and the M is about to block
    in a system call anyway. rdi, rbx and r12 are kept (rt_idlepoll's
